        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 111083869,
        "heading": "毒性",
        "text": "毒成分\nファルビチン、コンボルブリン\n毒部位\n種子\n毒症状\n嘔吐、下痢、腹痛、血圧低下",
        "truncated": false
      }
    },
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Ipomoea nil」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipomoea_nil#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1362248176,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "It is cultivated as an ornamental plant in many places, and the descendants of garden escapees now grow wild. Fast-growing and self-seeding, it may be used to hide unsightly fences or walls, and may also be used decoratively on trellises. The flowers are several centimeters wide and appear in various shades of blue, pink or rose, often with white stripes or edges or blends of colors. Common cultivars include 'Scarlet O'Hara', 'Early Call', and 'Rose Silk'.\nHybrids, for instance with I. purpurea, have been developed. Some of these have been given the name I. x imperialis (Imperial Japanese morning glory), which is not official. Cultivars include 'Sunrise Serenade'. Alternative nomenclatures include Ipomoea nil x imperialis, as in 'Cameo Elegance', or Ipomoea nil  'Imperialis'. It is a frost tender annual in colder areas or if placed in shaded area in the garden, but can be kept as a perennial if it is well managed and placed in full sun during the winter.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
